#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# groebner_basis

#% text
# `groebner_basis(polys, xs)` gives the reduced Groebner basis of the ideal generated by the polynomials `polys`, in the lexicographic order of the variables `xs`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# Other symbols are parameters (part of the coefficient field). The basis is reduced and each polynomial is normalized.

#% item
# In lexicographic order the last polynomials involve only the last variables, so the basis solves a system by back substitution.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
groebner_basis([x^2 + y^2 - 1, x - y], [x, y])
#% output
#= [-1 + 2*y^2, x - y]

#% cell
groebner_basis([x*y - 1, x^2 - y], [x, y])
#% output
#= [-1 + y^3, x - y^2]

#% cell
groebner_basis([x + y + z - 1, x - y, y - z], [x, y, z])
#% output
#= [-1 + 3*z, -1 + 3*y, -1 + 3*x]

#% subsection
# Applications

#% text
# A system without solutions has the basis `[1]`:

#% cell
groebner_basis([x^2 + 1, x], [x])
#% output
#= [1]

#% text
# Solving a system by its triangular basis:

#% cell
gb = groebner_basis([x^2 + y^2 - 5, x*y - 2], [x, y]); last(gb)
#% output
#= -5*y + 2*x + y^3

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# The order of the variables changes the basis:

#% cell
[groebner_basis([x^2 - y, x*y - 1], [x, y]), groebner_basis([x^2 - y, x*y - 1], [y, x])]
#% output
#= [[-1 + y^3, x - y^2], [-1 + x^3, y - x^2]]

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `eliminate`, `solve`, `resultant`, `polynomial_remainder`
